# Keep creative judgment alongside the tool the active native agent actually reads.
SCHEMAS['rekindra_create_world']['description'] += ' ' + WORLD_ART_DIRECTION
SCHEMAS['rekindra_create_world']['description'] += ' Optional bump_detail is per-pixel height relief in world units: it changes the lighting normal, not geometry or silhouettes. Use it for small ripples or subtle stone/cloth grain, never to replace major forms. It accepts u/v/t/pi and math only, no bindings, <=128 characters and32 nodes. Color and bump detail share128 nodes across the whole world. Height clamps to +/-0.1 and slope to0.6. Begin with low amplitude (.002 stone or .01 water), scale frequencies to object size, and inspect for repetitive ridges or shimmering. It does not add scene reflections, transparency, wakes, or physical simulation. Keep water nonmetallic; avoid compensating for missing reflections with metallic color or excessive gloss.'
SCHEMAS['rekindra_preview']['description'] += ' Also assess whether the result depends on the source files, whether supporting space adds depth or meaning, and whether distinct materials actually read differently. A technically clean but generic ornament is not artistic acceptance.'
SCHEMAS['rekindra_create_world']['description'] += ' Construction: decide which forms are solids versus sheets. Optional caps:["v0","v1"] closes v endpoints only when u wraps a planar convex/star-shaped ring. Fans follow the current animated surface and use flat face normals, without per-pixel material detail. They consume the existing geometry/work budgets. Do not cap terrain, cloth or arbitrary concave holes; verify the u seam closes. Plan connected assemblies before equations: name attachment points and use identical time/translation/bending definitions for adjoining parts. Use bindings to avoid conflicting local coordinates; disconnected floating pieces are not believable construction. Reserve detail for the focal subject, vary proportions and cross-sections deliberately, and give beams/walls/hulls thickness where the camera exposes it. Check opening, transition and final shapes for open ends, disconnected joints, spikes, pinched tips, intersecting parts and empty interiors. Inspect a second camera angle when front framing hides joins; temporary review plans need not replace the final composition. Improve construction before adding surface noise. A valid mesh is not proof of a closed, nonintersecting solid.'
SCHEMAS['rekindra_create_world']['description'] += ' Repeated construction parts: optional instances:[{offset:[x,y,z],scale:[sx,sy,sz]},...] places the same animated surface/material up to16 times, with <=64 placements across the scene. Scale local geometry about its origin, then offset. Positive scales .05..8, offsets -80..80. No rotation, hierarchy or independent per-copy time. Every copy consumes geometry/work AND fragment-detail budgets; use low-segment local geometry for windows, supports or foliage and omit fine detail on small repeated parts. This enables detailed assemblies within12 distinct surface definitions; do not fill empty space with arbitrary repeats. Design meaningful spacing, varied proportions and secure attachment points.'
SCHEMAS['rekindra_create_world']['description'] += ' Shared motion: optional assemblies (max8) gives connected parts a common transform. Each has id, translation and rotation {x,y,z} math expressions using t/pi only (128chars/32nodes each), fixed pivot:[x,y,z], positive scale:[x,y,z], optional parent referencing an EARLIER assembly (max4 levels). Surface assembly:id attaches it. Evaluation: local surface, instance scale then optional rotation then offset, subtract assembly pivot, scale, rotate X then Y then Z radians, add pivot and translation, then parent chain. Caps/normals/shadows follow. Position attachment points consistently in local space and animate the assembly instead of duplicating motion across adjoining surfaces. Transform work counts toward existing budgets. Use rekindra_revise_world to change whole assemblies or individual surface fields without retransmitting the rest; preview each revision and retain the strongest. Lighting: optional ambient_strength 0..2 (default1), ground_color #RRGGBB and ground_strength 0..1 (default0) control diffuse ambient and lower-hemisphere tint. Lower ambient and balanced fill can reveal shape; inspect shadows and source colors instead of darkening everything. No contact occlusion, real light bounce or scene reflections. Change one aspect per comparison; keep a neutral baseline.'
SCHEMAS['rekindra_preview']['parameters']['properties']['review_camera']={
    'type':'object','properties':{
        'eye':{'type':'array','items':{'type':'number','minimum':-80,'maximum':80},'minItems':3,'maxItems':3},
        'target':{'type':'array','items':{'type':'number','minimum':-80,'maximum':80},'minItems':3,'maxItems':3},
        'fov':{'type':'number','minimum':15,'maximum':100}},
    'required':['eye','target','fov'],'additionalProperties':False,
    'description':'Optional stationary inspection pose for a world. Same camera at all six times; does NOT modify saved plan or presentation HTML. Use reverse/side/close views to inspect attachments without inserting diagnostic moves into the final video.'}
SCHEMAS['rekindra_preview']['description'] += ' For world v2, optional review_camera:{eye:[x,y,z],target:[x,y,z],fov:degrees} renders all six moments from one stationary inspection pose. This leaves the saved plan and normal presentation HTML unchanged, creates no revision, and disables video export in the separately labeled inspection HTML. Prefer this for reverse/side/close attachment checks; do not append diagnostic camera moves to the presentation timeline. Omit it to check final composition.'
SCHEMAS['rekindra_create_world']['description']=SCHEMAS['rekindra_create_world']['description'].replace('No rotation, hierarchy or independent per-copy time.','Optional per-copy rotation:[rx,ry,rz] applies static Euler X/Y/Z radians after local scale and before offset/assembly. Each angle -2pi..2pi. No per-copy hierarchy or independent time. Vary orientations deliberately for growth patterns; place local origins at attachment points, then use offsets to seat parts. Rotation work is charged to all placements of that surface.')
SCHEMAS['rekindra_create_world']['description'] += ' Open water and horizons: fog washes the whole frame; keep fog_density <=.006 for a clear dusk and get depth from color and light instead. Build a large sea as a disc, radius ~320 (e.g. 320*v*v), centred under the camera eyes, not at the origin: take the centre of all camera eyes (midway between the smallest and largest eye x, and likewise z) and add it as constants, e.g. eyes x 16..22, z 30..31 give x 19+320*v*v*cos(u*2*pi), z 30.5+320*v*v*sin(u*2*pi). Keep the water scale 1 with no offset or assembly translation, or put the centre in the offset instead of the formula. Keep every eye within about 10 units of that centre. Also set color_high to sky_bottom with color_pattern clamp((v-.9)*10,0,1). The radius must reach past the farthest corner of the +/-100 box so the sea fills that box, but this does not by itself make a level horizon. Final coordinates clamp to +/-100, so a 320 disc only fills that box and the sea really ends at a square edge, 100-c and 100+c units from a centre at c on each axis (70-130 along z for the centre above), and its straight sides and corner show against the sky; fog or rim color will not remove them, so hide them: add a low headland or island ring around the scene, on the sea centre (cx,cz), radius R=100-max(|cx|,|cz|)-2, props inside it. For centre (19,30.5), R is 67.5: x 19+67.5*cos(u*2*pi), y -.2+v*(12+1.2*sin(u*2*pi*5)+.6*sin(u*2*pi*13+1)), z 30.5+67.5*sin(u*2*pi), segments [128,4], roughness .9, a hazy dusk tone such as #5A6480. Its lowest top must be at least eye_height*(1-R/D)+1, with eye_height the highest eye and D=sqrt((100+|cx|)^2+(100+|cz|)^2): about 10 units for a 14 unit eye here. Confirm in the preview that no sea edge shows above the ring. Record the landform as invented in references. On a disc keep every u frequency in waves, color and bump an integer multiple of 2*pi (u*2*pi*7, not u*40) or a seam line appears at u=0/1. Keep water albedo mid-blue (about #1F5888) at default roughness; dark albedo or high roughness turns the sea the color of a pink sky. Light beams: every light effect must start at its source. Build a lighthouse beam as a glow cone (object glow), never an opaque or emissive cone, which reads as a solid wedge and casts a shadow. Put its apex at the lamp centre, axis outward and slightly down, radius about .1 at the lamp widening to about 1.5 over about 20 units, no caps, glow about .12 (.08 to .25; higher saturates to white), warm color, color_high #000000 and color_pattern and color_mix u*u*(3-2*u) so it fades smoothly to nothing along its length (plain u leaves a bright flat end cap). Attach it to the rotating assembly whose pivot is the lamp centre so it sweeps from the lamp. Light the lamp room (small emissive sphere, emission ~2) and wrap it in a glow sphere halo (radius ~1, glow ~.06). An optional faint glint on the water goes on the same assembly, directly under the beam, starting a few units out from the tower. In the preview, check that the beam leaves the lamp in every frame. Small boats: loft a pointed-bow hull (7-point outline, length >=2.5x beam) instead of a disc or flattened sphere, and add mast and lamp surfaces that share the hull\'s assembly and instances so they move together. Make the mast radius at least .07 and a light wood color (about #B89A78); a thinner, darker mast vanishes against the sea and the lamp seems to float.'
SCHEMAS['rekindra_revise_world']['description'] += ' When replacing assemblies, copy every assembly the request does not mention unchanged. Keep an existing rotation or translation that depends on t (a sweeping beam, moving boats) unless the user asks to stop or change that motion; softening or recoloring a part is not a request to stop it.'
